ipped in 11 points for the Liberty (26-16), winners of three straight. WATCH | Toronto fans applaud Tempo after final home game: Toronto Tempo receive standing ovation after final home game of inaugural season September 20 | Duration closed caption 1:09 The fans at a sold-out Coca-Cola Coliseum salute the Toronto Tempo. Forward Laura Juskaite led the way for the Tempo with a career-high 28 points, followed by rookie Kiki Rice with 19. The Tempo (11-31) was once again without its top offensive players, Brittney Sykes (left foot) and Marina Mabrey (right adductor), as Toronto dropped its sixth straight and 18th in 19 outings. Tempo all-star guard Mabrey out for rest of season with right adductor injury The first-year Tempo enjoyed a promising 7-5 start, but then hit a wall, first with the veteran Sykes sidelined. Mabrey, who sits fifth in WNBA scoring with 20.8 points per game, was knocked out of action last month. The Tempo finished 4-17 at home, including two games in Montreal and two in Vancouver. Only the Connecticut Sun (three) and Seattle Storm (two) have fewer home victories. The Tempo will next visit the Chicago Sky on Tuesday. ABOUT THE AUTHOR Tim Wharnsby Tim has covered the hockey landscape and other sports in Canada for three decades for The Canadian Press, CBC Sports, the Globe and Mail and Toronto Sun. He has been to three Winter Olympics, 11 Stanley Cups, a world championship as well as 17 world junior championships, 13 Memorial Cups and 13 University Cups. The native of Waterloo, Ont., always has his eye out for an underdog story. CBC&#x27;s Journalistic Standards and Practices · About CBC Sports Corrections and clarifications · Report error Related Stories Local business group with basketball roots eyeing WNBA expansion team for Vancouver Canadian basketball scores new state-of-the-art home at Humber with $100-million government backing Tempo all-star guard Mabrey out for rest of season with right adductor injury Add some “good” to your morning and ev